#!/usr/bin/env bash
# UserPromptSubmit hook: one line of context on every prompt, so the project's
# skills are in front of the model at the moment it decides what to do -- not
# 200 lines earlier in CLAUDE.md. Output of a UserPromptSubmit hook is added to
# the conversation as context.
cat <<'MSG'
Project rule: start by running `bash tests/orient.sh` (one call, ~0.3s: structure, security, navigation, tests and their covers, coverage, lint, app state) instead of exploring by hand. Before building or changing any feature, invoke the Skill tool with `test-first-delivery` (failing test first). Before creating a module or placing documents: `module-structure`. Before writing a microflow: `naming-and-captions` -- every decision AND every action (retrieve, create, change, commit, delete, call, show page, set) needs a business `@caption`; the gate's naming check fails on a missing one or on the Mendix default. If the Skill tool does not list them -- it lists what existed when the session STARTED, so skills installed since are invisible to it -- read exactly those three files instead, `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, and nothing else up front. Do not sweep the other SKILL.md files before starting: entity, page, microflow and navigation syntax is a lookup at the moment you need it (`./mxcli syntax <topic>`, then `./mxcli check <script>.mdl -p <app>.mpr --references` before every exec). One session that read twelve of them first spent 6.5 minutes and 36 commands before it ran anything. Run the new test and watch it FAIL before implementing: `bash tests/gate.sh --only <feature> --boot-if-needed` (it starts the app if nothing is running). While you iterate run that same ONE script; when it goes red the gate prints the facts (rows, sessions, access rules) under the failure by itself. A feature is not done until `bash tests/gate.sh` (suite + mx check + lint + coverage, one call) ends in `DONE`.
MSG
